## Unnecessary Re-renders in React

### 1. Reference Equality

React uses `===` to compare props. Primitives compare by value, but **objects and functions compare by identity** — a new reference is created on every render even if the contents are identical.

```jsx
// Re-renders Child on every Parent render — new object reference each time
function Parent() {
  return <Child style={{ color: 'red' }} onClick={() => doSomething()} />;
}
```

Every render of `Parent` creates a brand-new `{}` and `() => {}`, so from React's perspective the props always changed.

---

### 2. React.memo, useMemo, useCallback

| Tool | What it memoizes | Use when |
|---|---|---|
| `React.memo` | Component output | Child receives stable props but re-renders due to parent updates |
| `useMemo` | A computed value | Expensive calculation, or object/array passed as prop |
| `useCallback` | A function reference | Function passed as prop to a memoized child |

**`React.memo`** — wraps a component, skips re-render if props are shallowly equal:
```jsx
const Child = React.memo(({ value }) => <div>{value}</div>);
```

**`useMemo`** — stabilizes an object/array reference or memoizes a heavy computation:
```jsx
const config = useMemo(() => ({ theme: 'dark', size: 'lg' }), []);
```

**`useCallback`** — stabilizes a function reference:
```jsx
const handleClick = useCallback(() => doSomething(id), [id]);
```

> **Rule of thumb:** don't memoize by default. Profile first. `useMemo`/`useCallback` have their own overhead and add cognitive noise. They pay off when (a) a child is wrapped in `React.memo`, or (b) the computation is measurably expensive.

---

### 3. Context Provider Pitfalls

A context value that's a new object on every render causes **every consumer to re-render**, regardless of whether the data changed.

```jsx
// Bad — new object on every render of App
function App() {
  const [user, setUser] = useState(null);
  return (
    <UserContext.Provider value={{ user, setUser }}>
      {children}
    </UserContext.Provider>
  );
}
```

**Two fixes:**

**Option A — memoize the value:**
```jsx
const value = useMemo(() => ({ user, setUser }), [user]);
```

**Option B (preferred) — split into separate contexts** so that consumers of stable data (e.g. `setUser`) don't re-render when `user` changes:
```jsx
<UserDispatchContext.Provider value={setUser}>
  <UserStateContext.Provider value={user}>
    {children}
  </UserStateContext.Provider>
</UserDispatchContext.Provider>
```

---

### 4. Before / After

**Before — `ExpensiveList` re-renders every time `Parent` renders, even if `items` didn't change:**

```jsx
function Parent({ items }) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const filtered = items.filter(i => i.active);     // new array every render
  const handleSelect = (id) => selectItem(id);       // new function every render

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>
      <ExpensiveList items={filtered} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect }) { /* ... */ }
```

**After — `ExpensiveList` only re-renders when `items` actually changes:**

```jsx
function Parent({ items }) {
  const [count, setCount] = useState(0);

  const filtered = useMemo(
    () => items.filter(i => i.active),
    [items]                                          // stable reference when items unchanged
  );

  const handleSelect = useCallback((id) => selectItem(id), []); // stable function

  return (
    <>
      <button onClick={() => setCount(c => c + 1)}>Count: {count}</button>
      <ExpensiveList items={filtered} onSelect={handleSelect} />
    </>
  );
}

const ExpensiveList = React.memo(function ExpensiveList({ items, onSelect }) {
  /* now skipped entirely when count changes */
});
```

The three pieces work together: `React.memo` opts the component into prop comparison, `useMemo` stabilizes the array reference, and `useCallback` stabilizes the function. Remove any one of them and the optimization collapses.
